  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe key insight is to use a multi-resolution optimization combined with a novel gradient smoothing technique to escape local minima. Unlike the original approach that strictly followed a two-phase optimization, we iteratively cyclically optimize across two scales (n=95 and n=100 samples) using gradient descent with adaptive Lagrange multipliers. This prevents premature convergence by maintaining tension between minimizing the overlap penalty and satisfying the sum constraint. The gradient smoothing is achieved by exponentiating shifted correlations and normalizing, producing continuous gradients even near maxima. This approach directly addresses the bottleneck of local minima trapping by leveraging scale-switching and gradient continuity.\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables:\n- h(k): 0 \u2264 h(k) \u2264 1 for all k, with \u2211h dx = 1\n- dx = 2.0/n_points (fixed domain scaling)\n- C5(h) = max_{k} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (correlation-based metric)\n\nObjective:\nmin C5(h) s.t. \u2211h dx = 1 (integral constraint)\n\nOptimization formulation:\nWe reformulate with Lagrange parameters (\u03b1, \u03b2):\nf(h, \u03b1, \u03b2) = \u03b1 * max_k C_k + \u03b2 * (sum(h) - n/2)**2\nThis combines the overlap penalty and constraint tracking in a differentiable framework.\n\nGradient computation via:\n1. Smoothed max calculation: Subtract max then exp normalize\n2. Differentiated correlation contributions for each pixel\n3. Constraint-aware gradient: Sparse contributions from positive/negative lags\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n- Read input resolution n_orig (95 samples)\n- Fix initial parent to satisfy integral constraint\n- c5_max = max(correlation * dx)\n\n2. Phase 1: Adaptive Lagrange tuning\n   For \u03b1 in [0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0] (inversion sequence):\n      \u03b2 = 0.1 * \u03b1  # Balancing parameter\n      Run gradient descent at n=95 and n=100\n      Track current best solution and time  \n      \u03b1_inversion = 1.0 / \u03b1  # Multiplicative inversion\n\n3. Phase 2: Resolution morphing\n   Interpolate parent to new resolution n_new (90/110 boundary)\n   Scale Lagrange parameters (\u03b1, \u03b2) by resolution\n   Repair and evaluate periodic transfers\n\n4. Phase 3: Random re-starts\n   Periodically crash-gradient and spawn new topology\n   Global repair preserves feasibility\n   Temperature schedule adjusts restart magnitude\n\n5. Stopping conditions:\n   - Time expiration (local gradient < 1e-8, constraint residual < 1e-4, and absence of improvement)\n   - Solution fixation (same solution detects >95% of time)\n\nMathematical guarantees:\n- Gradient descent proceeds with adaptive damping\n- Smoothed max maintains differentiability\n- Temperature schedules provide entropy to prevent stagnation\n- Reprojection ensures continuous L2 projection of feasible solutions\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Primary: Multi-resolution gradient descent (95->100 samples)\n2. Backup: Morphological stretching (temporal interpolation)\n3. Escaping: Periodic crash-gradient restarts with topology change\n4. Elasticity: Temperature schedule [0.01, 0.1, 1.0] adjusts flexibility\n5. Diversity: Lagrange inversion sequence for full search space coverage\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Cycle time: O(n) dominated by gradient evaluations (O(n\u00b2)\n- Memory: Linear storage (samples array)\n- Time allocation: Progressive reduction of \u03b1 prevarication\n- Staging:\n  - 40% for primary optimization\n  - 25% for resolution trials\n  - 35% for fallback restarts\n- Success checkpoint: Quantized validation at integral points\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Repair all candidates via L\u221e projection\n- Re-evaluate C5_max independently\n- Verify: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, \u2211h dx=1\n- Maximize solution entropy vs. clone ratio\n- Timely completion: Exit safer than failure\n- Error recovery: Second-order exploration fallback\n- Format: (h_final, c5_bound, n_points), all validated",
